From a9c902b701caeabf6fd396e8bf1496bce156fa64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 21:00:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Host places first, then player 2 - and take both humans off
 the AI

Why placing a castle did nothing, wherever you clicked: a mission's second
player is an AI opponent, and mission 1's is. The AI placed player 1's castle
itself within moments of the start, so has_castle() went true and every human
placement was refused from then on - can_build_castle's first test. Nothing to
do with position. net_begin now clears the AI bit on players 0 and 1 before a
single tick runs, on both machines identically, so the two humans drive their
own sides and nothing else does.

Castle placement is now sequenced, as Bob asked: player 0 chooses, then player 1,
who can see where player 0 went and answer it. Play proper begins once both have
one.

This costs no traffic and needs no new message. Whose turn it is, is read off the
simulation - who does not yet have a castle - and both machines are running the
same simulation, so both reach the same answer on the same tick without being
told. The rule is enforced in the COMMAND HANDLER, where both machines run it;
the check at the click is only a courtesy so the button does not feel dead. If
the click were the only check, a command that slipped through on one machine
would build a castle there and nowhere else, which is the whole class of bug
this design exists to avoid.

The status line says whose turn it is on both machines.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DFktm5o1kLnS2Pgvyp8tD4
